1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,665
It's two in the morning.

2
00:00:03,756 --> 00:00:07,380
The bakery closed six hours ago, but Maria is still awake.

3
00:00:07,726 --> 00:00:10,043
A storefront just came up for lease on Route 1,

4
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:12,360
and she has one big decision to make.

5
00:00:12,707 --> 00:00:16,064
If she gets it wrong, she could lose everything she's built.

6
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:20,540
Yizhan: Small businesses make decisions like this all the time, and usually alone.

7
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:24,260
Big companies have a board of directors, analysts, consultants,

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,200
whose whole job is to make decisions for them and push back on bad decisions.

9
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:30,920
A small business has a search bar,

10
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,440
and a regular AI chatbot that tends to agree with anything you say

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,000
and that you already believe in.

12
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:40,060
That feels good, but it's not advice. It's an echo.

13
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:44,160
What owners need is someone to challenge them before the money is spent.

14
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,067
Introducing Crucible.

15
00:00:46,107 --> 00:00:49,507
I'm Yizhan. With my teammates Tiffany and Ruijie, we built Crucible.

16
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:53,247
A crucible is a vessel where metal is melted under extreme heat,

17
00:00:53,287 --> 00:00:56,887
so the impurities burn off and only the strongest metal remains.

18
00:00:56,927 --> 00:00:59,007
That's what we do with ideas.

19
00:00:59,107 --> 00:01:01,987
Crucible gives every business owner a board of AI advisors

20
00:01:02,027 --> 00:01:04,967
that argue your decision under extreme pressure,

21
00:01:05,007 --> 00:01:07,947
until only the good answer survives.

22
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:09,207
Meet your board.

23
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:10,487
Here's your board.

24
00:01:10,807 --> 00:01:13,307
The Strategist thinks in years, not weeks.

25
00:01:13,347 --> 00:01:16,787
He asks, where does this decision take you in the long term?

26
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:21,087
The Realist lives in the numbers: cash, time, staffing.

27
00:01:21,127 --> 00:01:23,587
He asks, can you actually pull this off?

28
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:27,527
Now the Expert knows your industry: benchmarks, trends,

29
00:01:27,567 --> 00:01:30,187
and what has worked for businesses like yours.

30
00:01:30,227 --> 00:01:33,367
He asks, what does experience in your industry say?

31
00:01:33,407 --> 00:01:37,167
The Devil's Advocate has one job: find the flaw.

32
00:01:37,207 --> 00:01:39,867
Every assumption you make, it attacks.

33
00:01:40,047 --> 00:01:43,207
Now back to the Chairman, who runs the entire meeting.

34
00:01:43,247 --> 00:01:45,027
It doesn't take anyone's side.

35
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:49,787
He hears the entire debate, weighs the evidence, and makes the final call.

36
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:54,333
The AI-Tools Expert knows which AI products are real,

37
00:01:54,373 --> 00:01:56,960
what they cost, and what they plug into.

38
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,141
Before anyone speaks, a Researcher searches the web for the facts

39
00:02:00,181 --> 00:02:04,595
they'll all argue from, each one numbered and linked to its source.

40
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:06,793
Back to Maria.

41
00:02:07,395 --> 00:02:10,079
She opens Crucible and types her question:

42
00:02:10,681 --> 00:02:14,479
should I open a second location, or invest in delivery first?

43
00:02:14,825 --> 00:02:16,975
She's leaning toward the new store.

44
00:02:17,173 --> 00:02:20,697
Then she adds what an advisor would ask: about forty-two thousand

45
00:02:20,737 --> 00:02:25,283
dollars a month in revenue, ninety thousand in savings, six months of runway,

46
00:02:25,323 --> 00:02:28,448
and no manager, because she bakes every morning herself.

47
00:02:28,609 --> 00:02:30,433
And she convenes the board.

48
00:02:32,966 --> 00:02:35,399
First, the Researcher goes to the web.

49
00:02:35,439 --> 00:02:39,899
It comes back with thirteen facts, and twelve are verified with a source:

50
00:02:40,245 --> 00:02:42,743
build-out costs for bakery space,

51
00:02:42,834 --> 00:02:47,531
College Park rents from LoopNet, Prince George's County food-service permit rules,

52
00:02:47,571 --> 00:02:50,777
and the commission delivery apps take on every order.

53
00:02:51,976 --> 00:02:55,112
Every advisor now argues from the same evidence.

54
00:02:56,266 --> 00:03:01,260
Round one. Each advisor gives an opening position, without seeing the others.

55
00:03:01,356 --> 00:03:03,394
Industry Expert: Don't open Route 1 now.

56
00:03:03,434 --> 00:03:06,796
A second store needs far more than $90,000,

57
00:03:06,836 --> 00:03:11,273
and your first store has no manager while you bake every morning.

58
00:03:11,376 --> 00:03:16,126
Strategist: A second store would drain the runway that protects your first store.

59
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:22,314
Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand cheaply.

60
00:03:22,416 --> 00:03:25,476
Devil's Advocate: Opening a second store with $90,000 risks

61
00:03:25,516 --> 00:03:28,664
your first store, which pays for everything.

62
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:32,720
Realist: A second store would likely cost far more than your $90,000,

63
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:36,918
and you would need a certified food manager on site at both places.

64
00:03:37,016 --> 00:03:39,890
AI-Tools Expert: Add commission-free Square Online delivery now,

65
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:43,403
then reconsider a smaller space in 6 to 12 months.

66
00:03:43,506 --> 00:03:46,647
Every seat pushes back, even the Devil's Advocate,

67
00:03:46,687 --> 00:03:49,301
whose job is to attack the owner's leaning.

68
00:03:49,764 --> 00:03:51,890
An echo would have said yes.

69
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:54,161
Round two.

70
00:03:54,763 --> 00:03:56,917
Now the advisors answer each other by name,

71
00:03:56,957 --> 00:03:59,467
and the Devil's Advocate turns on the room.

72
00:04:03,763 --> 00:04:08,518
Devil's Advocate: Oppose waiting. Delivery won't fix a near-capped store.

73
00:04:08,558 --> 00:04:11,420
The Route 1 space may be leased quickly,

74
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:16,338
and nothing shows a comparable space will appear in 6 to 12 months.

75
00:04:16,499 --> 00:04:20,228
Waiting trades a known option for a guess.

76
00:04:20,323 --> 00:04:23,766
Strategist: Right, and the lock-in is the real cost.

77
00:04:23,806 --> 00:04:28,707
But a Route 1 lease might be negotiable on term or guarantee.

78
00:04:28,868 --> 00:04:31,679
That's something an attorney should test.

79
00:04:31,783 --> 00:04:36,638
AI-Tools Expert: Own-channel delivery still costs the $6.99 courier fee, plus card fees.

80
00:04:36,984 --> 00:04:40,910
Charge the customer the delivery fee, or small orders lose money.

81
00:04:41,013 --> 00:04:43,221
And the Realist changes its mind.

82
00:04:43,323 --> 00:04:47,393
Realist: The Strategist's point that delivery tests are cheap and reversible,

83
00:04:47,433 --> 00:04:51,816
plus the Devil's Advocate's point that a 5-to-10-year lease locks in costs,

84
00:04:51,856 --> 00:04:55,515
moved me from conditional to oppose on signing now.

85
00:04:55,613 --> 00:04:57,612
Nobody won by being loudest.

86
00:04:58,376 --> 00:05:01,571
The weak arguments burned off, and the plan got sharper:

87
00:05:01,662 --> 00:05:06,180
not never, but not yet, and here's how to find out.

88
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:09,687
Then the Chairman weighs both rounds.

89
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:13,758
Chairman: Pilot. Don't sign Route 1 now.

90
00:05:13,798 --> 00:05:16,563
Hire a manager and test Square delivery.

91
00:05:16,663 --> 00:05:19,904
Seventy-eight percent confident, with the reasons spelled out.

92
00:05:20,065 --> 00:05:23,078
A second site likely costs a hundred and fifty to five

93
00:05:23,118 --> 00:05:27,067
hundred thousand dollars, against ninety thousand in savings.

94
00:05:27,228 --> 00:05:29,668
It would need its own certified food manager.

95
00:05:29,829 --> 00:05:33,360
And a five-to-ten-year lease may carry a personal guarantee.

96
00:05:33,563 --> 00:05:35,492
The dissent stays on the record:

97
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:39,997
the Devil's Advocate warns that waiting may lose Route 1 for good.

98
00:05:40,193 --> 00:05:44,008
And Maria gets steps she can start this week: ask the landlord

99
00:05:44,048 --> 00:05:48,303
for the real lease terms, pull her Square reports to find her true profit,

100
00:05:48,343 --> 00:05:50,474
turn on Square Online for pickup,

101
00:05:50,514 --> 00:05:54,661
and book a free session with the Maryland Small Business Development Center.

102
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:59,479
The board advises. Maria decides.

103
00:05:59,519 --> 00:06:03,433
She can accept the recommendation, or override it and write down why.

104
00:06:03,524 --> 00:06:05,627
Tonight, she can finally sleep.

105
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:10,034
And it works for any decision.

106
00:06:10,236 --> 00:06:13,249
A dental office in Silver Spring asks whether an AI

107
00:06:13,289 --> 00:06:16,101
receptionist should answer and record its calls.

108
00:06:16,262 --> 00:06:20,587
The board catches Maryland's rule that everyone on a call must agree to recording,

109
00:06:20,627 --> 00:06:23,682
and the HIPAA agreement the vendor would have to sign.

110
00:06:25,086 --> 00:06:30,173
An HVAC shop in Frederick wants to text a promotion to every past customer.

111
00:06:30,264 --> 00:06:33,382
The board flags federal consent rules for marketing texts,

112
00:06:33,422 --> 00:06:36,814
and pilots a small batch of opted-in customers instead.

113
00:06:37,016 --> 00:06:38,625
Same board. Same fight.

114
00:06:38,665 --> 00:06:40,570
A clear answer every time.

115
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:43,980
Yizhan: Under the hood, each advisor is its own AI agent,

116
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:47,060
with a distinct role, goals and personality.

117
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:50,060
Crucible gives them the question and the owner's context,

118
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:55,660
then runs structured debate rounds, where each agent has to answer the others.

119
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:57,500
The full transcript goes to the Chairman,

120
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:01,100
who weighs the strongest arguments into a final decision.

121
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:04,260
OpenRouter gives a great benefit with AI agents,

122
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:07,280
where we can use multiple of them at the same time.

123
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:11,840
In code, Crucible is a graph of seven agents built with Google's

124
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:16,206
Agent Development Kit: a Researcher with live web search,

125
00:07:16,246 --> 00:07:20,195
five seats that run in parallel each round, and a Chairman.

126
00:07:20,797 --> 00:07:25,768
The seats run on Claude Haiku, the Researcher and Chairman on Claude Sonnet,

127
00:07:25,859 --> 00:07:30,980
all through OpenRouter, with a React front end and a FastAPI back end.

128
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:34,014
Every answer has to fit a strict schema.

129
00:07:34,054 --> 00:07:37,137
Facts carry numbers, the Realist's checks are pass,

130
00:07:37,177 --> 00:07:41,300
flag or block, and an advisor can rebut anyone but itself.

131
00:07:41,763 --> 00:07:44,196
The meeting streams to the browser one step at a time,

132
00:07:44,236 --> 00:07:46,130
so you can watch the board think.

133
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:47,975
And we test it.

134
00:07:48,321 --> 00:07:51,625
Ten business scenarios with legal traps planted in them,

135
00:07:51,665 --> 00:07:54,442
scored against a single plain AI prompt.

136
00:07:54,904 --> 00:07:59,771
In an early test on the dental case, the single prompt missed both planted risks.

137
00:07:59,862 --> 00:08:01,559
The board caught both.

138
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:08,080
Yizhan: Why it matters? Most small businesses can't afford consultants or a real board.

139
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:12,760
Crucible gives them the same pushback for the price of a question.

140
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:15,780
Next, we want owners to save their decisions,

141
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:21,120
ask follow-ups to any advisor, and build custom boards for their industry.

142
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,722
Over sixteen weeks, that becomes a decision history that records how

143
00:08:25,762 --> 00:08:29,727
each call turned out, follow-up questions for any advisor,

144
00:08:29,767 --> 00:08:34,580
boards tuned to each industry, real numbers from QuickBooks and Square,

145
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:38,760
and a hand-off to a Maryland SBDC advisor when the stakes are high.

146
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:41,530
And each meeting costs about ten cents.

147
00:08:43,067 --> 00:08:46,041
Crucible. Where decisions are forged.

148
00:08:46,132 --> 00:08:49,361
Built by Yizhan, Tiffany and Ruijie.
